/* Deep long-form expansions: Vamana, Kurma, Matsya, Garuda, Brahmanda. */
(() => {
  const D=window.SCRIPTURE_DETAIL_DATA=window.SCRIPTURE_DETAIL_DATA||{};
  const add=(name,sections,sources=[])=>{const key=`Purāṇa:${name}`,e=Object.assign({},D[name]||{},D[key]||{});D[key]=Object.assign({},e,{scholarlyDepth:'deep-v3',articleSections:[...(e.articleSections||[]),...sections],sources:[...(e.sources||[]),...sources]});};

  add('Vāmana Purāṇa',[
    {title:'Date of composition',paragraphs:[
      'The Vamana Purana’s title encourages an expectation of a Vishnu-avatara scripture, yet the received work is strongly Shaiva and regionally focused. This mismatch is one of the main clues to its textual history: the Vamana frame, older Purana myth and later sacred-geography material were assembled across different stages rather than composed as one thematic biography.',
      'Scholars have placed the principal received formation broadly in the early medieval period, often around the ninth to eleventh centuries, while allowing older inherited myth and later supplementation. Hazra’s chronology relies on religious geography, parallels and the historical profile of ritual material rather than the antiquity of the Vamana-Bali myth itself.',
      'Early printed editions contained about ninety-six chapters, while later common forms have roughly sixty-nine chapters plus a supplement. The supplement is absent from some Bengal manuscripts. A date assigned to a passage in that supplementary material therefore cannot automatically be applied to the shorter recension.',
      'The Saromahatmya, a twenty-eight-chapter sacred-geography block centred on Kurukshetra/Thanesar and neighbouring regions, is especially important for dating. It presupposes a mature network of tirthas whose textual sacralization belongs to the history of early-medieval pilgrimage.',
      'The Purana is thus layered in at least three ways: old Vamana and general Purana myths; a Shaiva-oriented early-medieval redaction; and regionally expandable pilgrimage or supplementary materials. Chapter number and recension are indispensable for chronology.'
    ]},
    {title:'Structure',paragraphs:[
      'The Vamana does not possess a universally stable book division. The most useful structural map is the relation between its short Vamana frame, long Shaiva mythic materials, Goddess narratives, cosmology and the Saromahatmya pilgrimage book.',
      'Chapter-count instability between older ninety-six-chapter prints and newer sixty-nine-chapter-plus-supplement forms shows that modern readers are dealing with at least two substantial editorial arrangements. A bare reference such as “Vamana Purana chapter 70” can therefore be meaningless without an edition.',
      'The Saromahatmya behaves as a coherent regional guide within the larger corpus. It maps lakes, rivers, forests and tirthas around Kurukshetra and Thanesar through origin stories and ritual fruits. Its twenty-eight chapters give the work a geographical centre far stronger than the Vamana title suggests.',
      'Shaiva narrative blocks—Sati, Parvati, Skanda, demon conflicts and sacred places—occupy much of the received Purana. Vishnu and Vamana remain significant, but the structural bulk belongs to a plural religious landscape with pronounced Shiva orientation.',
      'The work’s organization therefore illustrates Purana naming convention: a canonical title can preserve an old divine association even when later redaction makes different themes dominant.'
    ],subsections:[
      {title:'Vamana-Bali frame',paragraphs:[
        'Narada’s opening question about Vishnu’s dwarf manifestation gives the title its narrative legitimacy. The Vamana-Bali episode provides familiar Vaishnava cosmology of divine intervention, royal gift and cosmic sovereignty.',
        'Yet the story does not govern every subsequent chapter. It functions more like an authoritative portal into a wider Purana library than a plot whose consequences structure the entire book.'
      ]},
      {title:'Shaiva mythic body',paragraphs:[
        'The Sati-Parvati, Skanda and Shiva-related materials create a long Shaiva narrative environment. Their prominence makes the Vamana one of the best examples of why later sectarian labels should be based on actual contents rather than title alone.',
        'The mythology links naturally to regional sacred geography: Shiva’s actions establish places, while places become evidence of divine action.'
      ]},
      {title:'Saromahatmya',paragraphs:[
        'The Saromahatmya turns Kurukshetra from an epic battlefield name into a dense pilgrimage region. Lakes, streams, forests, shrines and ritual stations are connected through mythic etiology.',
        'Because Kurukshetra already carried Vedic and epic prestige, the mahatmya can layer new local sites onto an exceptionally old sacred reputation. Literary growth therefore works by intensifying rather than inventing sanctity from nothing.'
      ]}
    ]},
    {title:'Contents',paragraphs:[
      'The Vamana Purana’s main historical value lies in the interaction among old avatara myth, Shaiva theological narrative and the regional construction of Kurukshetra. Its contents show a canonical work becoming a pilgrimage scripture without surrendering the inherited name that first authorized it.'
    ],subsections:[
      {title:'Vamana and Bali — gift, sovereignty and divine scale',paragraphs:[
        'The dwarf avatara approaches Bali through the language of gift. A seemingly modest request for three steps becomes cosmic repossession when Vamana expands to span the worlds. The myth therefore turns dana and royal generosity into a test of who truly possesses sovereignty.',
        'Bali’s greatness is not erased by defeat. His willingness to honour his promise after recognizing Vishnu gives the story a devotional dimension: surrender can become the higher form of kingship.',
        'The episode’s antiquity across Sanskrit literature should be distinguished from its function here. In the Vamana Purana it authorizes a work whose later bulk is concerned with other gods and places.'
      ]},
      {title:'Sati, Parvati and Shiva',paragraphs:[
        'Shaiva narratives repeat the conflict between ascetic divinity and sacrificial-social order. Sati’s death, Parvati’s austerity and Shiva’s marriage move from rupture to cosmic reconciliation.',
        'Their presence in a Vamana-titled work is not accidental miscellany. The received redaction participates in a broad early-medieval world in which Purana titles could absorb competing sectarian myths under one canonical umbrella.'
      ]},
      {title:'Skanda and demon conflict',paragraphs:[
        'The birth of the war god and battles with demons connect divine family mythology to the restoration of cosmic order. Skanda’s martial role also fits the Kurukshetra environment in which heroic and sacred geography overlap.',
        'Comparative reading with Skanda, Shiva and Matsya Puranas reveals how the same mythic cycle is reconfigured by different redactors.'
      ]},
      {title:'Kurukshetra as layered sacred geography',paragraphs:[
        'The Saromahatmya maps a region already famous in Vedic ritual and Mahabharata memory. Rather than treating the battlefield alone as sacred, it multiplies tirthas across surrounding water bodies, forests and settlements.',
        'This multiplication changes the religious use of epic space. A pilgrim does not merely remember the war; one moves through a landscape where older sages, gods and ritual acts have left many local points of access.',
        'The text’s promised fruits—purification, ancestor benefit, merit and liberation—make geography actionable. Narrative memory becomes itinerary.'
      ]},
      {title:'Goddess and plural cult',paragraphs:[
        'Goddess traditions appear within the Shaiva-heavy corpus, adding another layer of regional and theological plurality. Sacred places can belong simultaneously to Shiva, Goddess and Vishnu networks.',
        'Such overlap is historically more realistic than a map divided into sealed sectarian zones. Pilgrimage often creates shared sacred territories even when texts rank deities differently.'
      ]},
      {title:'Cosmology and Purana continuity',paragraphs:[
        'Standard creation, cosmic cycles and inherited myth provide continuity with the Mahapurana genre. They frame the regionally specific material within universal time and space.',
        'The contrast in scale is important: a tiny local pond can acquire cosmic significance because the same text that describes world ages also explains what happened at its bank.'
      ]}
    ]},
    {title:'Theology',paragraphs:[
      'The received Vamana Purana is theologically plural with pronounced Shaiva emphasis. Vishnu’s Vamana supplies title and important myth, but Shiva’s stories and sacred places occupy greater continuous space.',
      'This demonstrates why sectarian classification should be descriptive rather than canonical: a title or later sattva-rajas-tamas label cannot substitute for reading the chapters.',
      'Sacred geography becomes a theological medium. Kurukshetra’s landscape holds multiple divine presences, making locality a form of revelation.',
      'Bali’s surrender provides a Vaishnava theology of devotion and gift, while Parvati’s tapas and Shiva myths offer distinct models of ascetic and divine power. The Purana does not fully systematize these voices.',
      'Ritual merit, pilgrimage and liberation coexist. The deity can be approached through gift, bath, vow, journey or narrative remembrance rather than one exclusive religious technology.'
    ]},
    {title:'Influences and reception',paragraphs:[
      'The Vamana’s strongest reception is tied to Kurukshetra sacred geography. The Saromahatmya contributed to the literary mapping of a region whose religious importance long predated the extant Purana.',
      'By connecting local sites to epic and Purana myths, the text helped make regional travel part of pan-Indian sacred history. Later pilgrimage guides inherit the same method.',
      'The title-content mismatch influenced modern scholarship because it exposed the inadequacy of classifying Puranas by nominal deity alone.',
      'Shaiva stories in the text circulate far beyond the Purana, and their shared versions help reconstruct a common mythic reservoir. Influence therefore cannot always be assigned in one direction.',
      'Print recension differences also shaped reception. A reader of a ninety-six-chapter edition and one using a sixty-nine-chapter plus supplement edition may encounter differently bounded “complete” Puranas.',
      'Modern regional histories of Thanesar and Kurukshetra use the Purana as literary evidence, but its sacred map must be compared with archaeology, inscription and other tirtha texts.'
    ]},
    {title:'Rites, dharma and social history',paragraphs:[
      'Pilgrimage dominates practical religion through bath, gift, vow, worship and repeated movement. The Saromahatmya implies a regional network of ritual specialists and travellers without directly documenting its historical economy.',
      'Epic prestige is ritualized. Kurukshetra becomes not only the location of remembered war but a field where ancestral and purificatory rites can be repeatedly performed.',
      'Shaiva and Goddess practices coexist with Vaishnava observance, showing a plural pilgrimage environment.',
      'Prescriptive dharma materials should be read as ideals. The book’s regional focus makes comparison with local inscriptions particularly valuable for testing institutional claims.',
      'The text also demonstrates how royal and martial memory can be domesticated into peaceful pilgrimage, gift and bathing centuries after the heroic narratives it invokes.'
    ]},
    {title:'Critical edition',paragraphs:[
      'The divergent ninety-six-chapter and sixty-nine-chapter-plus-supplement arrangements make recension identification essential. Supplements absent from Bengal witnesses cannot be assumed part of every Vamana tradition.',
      'The Saromahatmya should be studied as an internal unit whose manuscript history may differ from neighbouring chapters.',
      'There is no universally definitive stemmatic edition resolving the entire corpus. Printed Sanskrit witnesses remain the practical base for most scholarship.',
      'Cross-Purana comparison of Shaiva myths can identify shared source traditions, but similarity must be tested in Sanskrit rather than through plot summary alone.'
    ]},
    {title:'Further reading',paragraphs:[
      'Rocher and Hazra remain the principal orientation for structure and chronology. Regional scholarship on Kurukshetra should accompany them for the Saromahatmya.',
      'The text is most informative when read alongside Shiva, Skanda and Matsya rather than isolated as a “Vamana biography.”'
    ]}
  ],[{title:'Ludo Rocher — The Puranas',detail:'Standard Vamana Purana survey.'},{title:'R. C. Hazra — Studies in the Puranic Records',detail:'Important for early-medieval chronology.'}]);

  add('Kūrma Purāṇa',[
    {title:'Date of composition',paragraphs:[
      'The Kurma Purana is a composite early-medieval text whose major received form is often placed around the eighth century with later revision. Its importance lies precisely in the religious world implied by that date: Vaishnava, Shaiva and Goddess traditions are sufficiently developed to be negotiated inside one synthetic scripture.',
      'The Vishnu-Kurma frame is old, but the Ishvara Gita’s Shaiva philosophical discourse reflects a mature environment of Gita imitation and sectarian synthesis. Relative chronology should therefore distinguish the inherited avatara frame from the embedded teaching text.',
      'The received two-part structure, with around fifty-one chapters in Purvabhaga and forty-four to forty-six in Uttarabhaga depending edition, is more stable than many giant mahatmya Puranas but still contains layered didactic and pilgrimage units.',
      'Dharma, yoga and sacred-place chapters also have separate histories. Their relation to smriti, Mahabharata and Shaiva traditions can constrain dates more effectively than the canonical Kurma title.',
      'The text’s theological inclusivism should itself be historically situated. Identification or mutual subordination of Shiva and Vishnu is characteristic of a medieval environment negotiating powerful sectarian systems, not proof that all earlier Vaishnava and Shaiva traditions were doctrinally identical.'
    ]},
    {title:'Structure',paragraphs:[
      'The common Purvabhaga/Uttarabhaga division creates a strong two-part architecture. Purva supplies creation, divine narratives, Goddess and Shaiva-Vaishnava synthesis, dynasties, manvantaras and Vedic transmission. Uttara opens with the Ishvara Gita, proceeds to Vyasa Gita and then devotes many chapters to dharma, household and ascetic duties, śrāddha, expiation, pilgrimage, Narmada and finally dissolution.',
      'The placement of Ishvara Gita at the beginning of Uttarabhaga is structurally significant. The second half opens not with ordinary dharma but with Shiva teaching a high path of knowledge and yoga, after which the text descends into detailed social and ritual duties. Philosophy frames practice rather than appearing only as a final escape from it.',
      'Vyasa Gita and the subsequent dharma sequence make the book a dialogue among scriptural modes. Shiva can teach liberation, Vyasa can teach conduct and the Kurma-Vishnu frame can authorize both. The structure enacts theological synthesis.',
      'The concluding pilgrimage and dissolution chapters reverse the movement. After detailed social obligations, the reader moves through sacred geography and finally watches the cosmos dissolve. The two-part work thereby places everyday dharma within the largest metaphysical scale.'
    ],subsections:[
      {title:'Purvabhaga — cosmic synthesis',paragraphs:[
        'The first part establishes a universe in which Vishnu/Kurma, Shiva and Goddess traditions can be simultaneously authoritative. Creation and cosmic cycles supply the common field in which sectarian hierarchy can be negotiated.',
        'Narratives do not always produce perfect systematic agreement. Their juxtaposition is itself evidence of a redaction trying to encompass multiple sacred traditions.'
      ]},
      {title:'Ishvara Gita',paragraphs:[
        'The opening chapters of Uttarabhaga present Shiva as Ishvara teaching sages about supreme reality, prakriti and purusha, yoga, knowledge, devotion and his cosmic dance. The title deliberately recalls Bhagavad Gita authority.',
        'This is more than literary imitation. A Shaiva teaching enters a genre already associated with Krishna’s revelation, asserting that Shiva too can disclose a comprehensive path linking metaphysics, devotion and disciplined action.'
      ]},
      {title:'Vyasa Gita and the long dharma sequence',paragraphs:[
        'After high theology, Vyasa-associated instruction turns to water sipping, karmayoga, student discipline, householder duties, food, daily routines, śrāddha, impurity, homa, livelihood, gifts, forest life, renunciation and expiation.',
        'The sequence is almost a dharmashastra embedded in a Purana. Its placement shows how liberation teaching and social prescription can be imagined as complementary levels rather than competing genres.'
      ]},
      {title:'Tirtha and Narmada close',paragraphs:[
        'The later chapters move through holy places and Narmada material before cosmic dissolution. Pilgrimage becomes the spatial counterpart to the earlier social disciplines.',
        'The transition from river geography to pralaya is conceptually powerful: places promise merit within the world, but the world itself remains impermanent.'
      ]}
    ]},
    {title:'Contents',paragraphs:[
      'The Kurma Purana’s main subject is synthesis. It repeatedly asks how different forms of authority—Vishnu, Shiva, Goddess, Veda, yoga, household dharma and pilgrimage—can inhabit one sacred order without simply being flattened into sameness.'
    ],subsections:[
      {title:'Kurma and the Vaishnava frame',paragraphs:[
        'Vishnu’s tortoise manifestation supplies the textual identity and cosmic authority. The avatara associated with supporting the churning of the ocean is an apt emblem for a Purana that itself supports many religious traditions on one framework.',
        'Yet the frame does not guarantee exclusive Vaishnava theology. The text repeatedly grants Shiva a role of supreme teacher and accommodates Goddess traditions.'
      ]},
      {title:'Shiva as supreme teacher',paragraphs:[
        'The Ishvara Gita’s Shiva is not a subordinate deity briefly praised inside a Vaishnava book. He teaches ultimate reality, liberation and yoga. The text thereby makes sectarian hierarchy intentionally ambiguous or synthetic.',
        'Later interpreters can resolve the ambiguity differently—through identity of Shiva and Vishnu, hierarchy of forms or sectarian appropriation. The Purana’s historical value lies in preserving the negotiation itself.'
      ]},
      {title:'The Goddess and divine power',paragraphs:[
        'Goddess material adds a third major theological current. Shakti is connected with creation and divine manifestation, making the Purana’s synthesis more than a bilateral Vishnu-Shiva compromise.',
        'The presence of Goddess themes also links Kurma to wider early-medieval developments visible in Markandeya, Devi Bhagavata and Shaiva Tantric traditions.'
      ]},
      {title:'Dharma across life stages',paragraphs:[
        'The Uttarabhaga’s long social sequence gives unusually explicit treatment to student, householder, forest-dweller and renunciant. Food, purity, occupation, gift and śrāddha make religious life concrete.',
        'The progression does not imply every historical person passed mechanically through four stages. It expresses a Brahmanical normative model that the Purana seeks to integrate with yoga and liberation.'
      ]},
      {title:'Expiation and moral repair',paragraphs:[
        'Prayashchitta chapters assume that the normative order will be violated. Religion therefore requires techniques of repair rather than only rules of ideal conduct.',
        'The social significance is important: fault is classified, penance calibrated and ritual authority assigned, creating a system through which broken norm can be reintegrated.'
      ]},
      {title:'Pilgrimage and dissolution',paragraphs:[
        'The holy-place chapters and Narmada material make regional geography part of the same religious system as household law and yoga. One can pursue merit through disciplined residence, travel or ascetic realization.',
        'Pralaya then relativizes all three. The text closes by placing social order, sacred geography and cosmic manifestation within cycles whose ultimate ground lies beyond them.'
      ]}
    ]},
    {title:'Theology',paragraphs:[
      'Kurma theology is deliberately synthetic. Vishnu supplies the frame, Shiva supplies some of the highest teaching, Goddess power participates in creation and the text repeatedly moves toward a supreme reality capable of encompassing named divine forms.',
      'This should not be simplified into modern “all gods are exactly the same.” Medieval inclusivism can preserve hierarchy and sectarian language even while asserting deeper unity.',
      'The Ishvara Gita joins jnana, yoga and devotion. Shiva’s cosmic dance and lordship are not merely myth but expressions of metaphysical sovereignty.',
      'Karmayoga and dharma remain legitimate within this high theology. Social action can be disciplined without being ultimate, while renunciation and knowledge point beyond ordinary fruit.',
      'Pilgrimage, ritual and philosophical insight are therefore not separate religions inside the book. They are graded or intersecting practices in a synthetic sacred order.'
    ]},
    {title:'Influences and reception',paragraphs:[
      'The Ishvara Gita developed a life as an important Shaiva Gita text, demonstrating the prestige of the Bhagavad Gita form across sectarian traditions.',
      'Kurma’s theological synthesis became evidence for later interpreters seeking scriptural support for Shiva-Vishnu unity or for one deity’s supremacy interpreted through unity. The same text could therefore be cited in opposing sectarian arguments.',
      'Its dharma chapters contributed Purana authority to household and ascetic norms. Citation in later compendia helps date and reconstruct those sections.',
      'Narmada and tirtha materials connect it to wider pilgrimage-text networks, where similar place legends can migrate among Puranas.',
      'Modern scholarship values Kurma especially because it shows early-medieval sectarian boundaries as porous. Borrowing the Gita form is not evidence of lack of originality but of active competition within shared scriptural culture.',
      'The text also complicates simple sattva/rajas/tamas Purana classification: actual chapters reveal a far more entangled religious world.'
    ]},
    {title:'Rites, dharma and social history',paragraphs:[
      'Few Puranas integrate philosophical and normative material as visibly as the Kurma Uttarabhaga. Student discipline, household routine, śrāddha, purity, livelihood and renunciation can be read beside the Ishvara Gita in the same textual movement.',
      'Food and purity chapters reveal social boundary-making, but their prescriptive nature must be distinguished from observed practice.',
      'Gift and ancestral rites imply economies of patronage and lineage, while ascetic chapters imagine withdrawal from those same social relations.',
      'Expiation provides a technology for repairing breach, showing that norm is meaningful partly because systems exist for returning from violation.',
      'Pilgrimage broadens the social world beyond local household. Tirtha travel creates encounters among regional cults, priests and devotees that the legal-style chapters alone cannot capture.'
    ]},
    {title:'Critical edition',paragraphs:[
      'The Kurma has a comparatively manageable two-part structure but still lacks one universally dominant complete critical edition accepted across all scholarship. Chapter counts can vary, especially in Uttarabhaga.',
      'The Ishvara Gita should be collated as an embedded text because its relation to Bhagavad Gita and Shaiva sources is central to interpretation.',
      'Dharma passages should be compared with smriti and nibandha quotations; external citation can establish textual history more securely than theological assumptions.',
      'Pilgrimage units may possess independent recensional histories and should not be assumed coeval with the philosophical core.'
    ]},
    {title:'Further reading',paragraphs:[
      'Rocher provides the best general map. Detailed Ishvara Gita study should combine Sanskrit comparison with histories of medieval Shaiva philosophy and Gita literature.',
      'The dharma sequence deserves to be read beside Dharmashastra rather than dismissed as miscellaneous Purana material; it is one of the keys to the work’s synthetic ambition.'
    ]}
  ],[{title:'Ludo Rocher — The Puranas',detail:'Standard Kurma survey.'},{title:'Ancient Indian Tradition and Mythology — Kurma Purana translation',detail:'Useful for the two-part chapter sequence.'}]);

  add('Matsya Purāṇa',[
    {title:'Date of composition',paragraphs:[
      'The Matsya Purana preserves some comparatively old Purana materials but reached its surviving encyclopedic form through long revision. Modern scholarship often places important core strata in the Gupta or early post-Gupta world while recognizing additions continuing into the early medieval period and beyond.',
      'The flood-Manu narrative itself is far older than the extant Purana recension. Its presence at the opening therefore cannot date the 290–291 chapters that follow. The same caution applies to genealogies and avatara myths inherited from a shared epic-Purana reservoir.',
      'Architectural and iconographic chapters provide stronger relative chronology because they presuppose developed temple forms and technical vocabularies. Some elements may reflect Gupta-period traditions, while other details fit a mature early-medieval built environment. The date a building concept existed and the date it entered the Matsya remain distinct.',
      'Vrata, dāna and sacred-geography blocks likewise accumulated. Their relation to later ritual digests and regional traditions can be used to identify stages of growth.',
      'The received work is therefore best described as an old Purana frame expanded into an early-medieval encyclopedia of ritual, polity and built religion.'
    ]},
    {title:'Structure',paragraphs:[
      'Common editions contain about 290 or 291 chapters in one long sequence rather than large skandha divisions. The final chapter itself provides an index-like retrospective summary, confirming the extraordinary range: Manu and the flood, creation, genealogies, ancestors, vratas, gifts, pilgrimage, Shaiva myths, royal duty, omens, architecture, images, temples and future kings.',
      'The absence of large books does not mean absence of structure. Subject clusters are visible: the flood and creation opening; dynasties and ancestral rites; long vrata and gift sequences; pilgrimage and cosmography; Shaiva myth cycles; rajadharma; technical architecture and iconography; later dynastic and concluding materials.',
      'The work’s architecture is itself a history of Purana expansion. Older mythic and genealogical strata provide a framework into which ritual manuals and technical shastras can be incorporated.',
      'Matsya as speaker creates continuity. The fish who preserves knowledge through cosmic flood becomes an ideal narrator for a book whose later redactors imagine preserving many kinds of knowledge inside one scripture.'
    ],subsections:[
      {title:'Flood and recreation',paragraphs:[
        'Manu protects the small fish that continually outgrows its container; the fish reveals the coming destruction and instructs preservation. The story links compassion, recognition of divinity and survival of knowledge.',
        'After flood comes re-creation. The narrative makes preservation itself a sacred task, an apt literary charter for an encyclopedic Purana that stores genealogical, ritual and technical memory.'
      ]},
      {title:'Genealogy and ancestors',paragraphs:[
        'Solar and lunar lines, Pitrs, śrāddha and dynastic traditions give the early-middle chapters a classical Purana profile. Family history is embedded in cosmic history.',
        'Ancestral rites make genealogy practical: descent is not only remembered but ritually maintained through offerings.'
      ]},
      {title:'Vrata and dāna encyclopedia',paragraphs:[
        'Long sequences describe fasts, sacred lunar days, deity observances and extraordinary gifts such as symbolic mountains. The ritual calendar turns recurring time into a field of merit.',
        'Dāna chapters also display the imaginative economy of Purana merit, where symbolic objects can represent cosmic abundance and royal patronage becomes religious world-making.'
      ]},
      {title:'Shaiva narrative cycle',paragraphs:[
        'The Matsya contains substantial Shiva-Parvati-Skanda and demon-war material despite its Vishnu avatara title. The cycle includes Kama’s burning, Parvati’s austerity, marriage, Skanda’s birth and Taraka’s defeat.',
        'This shared mythology links Matsya closely to Vamana, Shiva and Skanda traditions and demonstrates again that Purana titles do not enforce sectarian exclusivity.'
      ]},
      {title:'Raja-dharma and omens',paragraphs:[
        'Royal duty, expedition, dreams and omens translate cosmic order into political decision. A king must govern not only through law but through interpretation of signs, ritual timing and counsel.',
        'These chapters show the Purana meeting niti, astrology and courtly practical knowledge in the same way its architecture section meets shilpashastra.'
      ]},
      {title:'Vastu, images and temple architecture',paragraphs:[
        'The technical chapters describe site, plan, palace and temple forms, image characteristics, proportions, mandapas and related architectural knowledge. They are among the most frequently cited Purana sources in Indian art history.',
        'Their value is comparative rather than legislative. Surviving temples rarely follow one textual recipe exactly; regional schools and artisan traditions vary. Agreement and divergence with Matsya both illuminate the history of architecture.'
      ]}
    ]},
    {title:'Contents',paragraphs:[
      'The Matsya Purana’s contents form a long meditation on preservation and order. The flood saves life and knowledge; genealogies preserve lineage; śrāddha preserves ancestral relation; vrata and gift preserve moral order; kingship preserves society; temple and image preserve divine presence in built form.'
    ],subsections:[
      {title:'Manu and Matsya',paragraphs:[
        'The opening relationship begins with a tiny creature asking protection. Manu’s care is rewarded by revelation: what seemed vulnerable is the power capable of saving the world. The reversal gives the flood story an ethical as well as cosmological dimension.',
        'The ark or vessel of preservation carries seeds, sages and knowledge across dissolution. Purana memory itself can be imagined as analogous cargo: traditions survive because they are collected before catastrophe.'
      ]},
      {title:'Creation and Samkhya-like cosmology',paragraphs:[
        'Post-flood creation uses categories of elements, gunas and cosmic evolution found across Puranas. The world emerges through ordered differentiation and returns through dissolution.',
        'This philosophical vocabulary provides a theoretical background for a book otherwise crowded with concrete ritual and technical detail.'
      ]},
      {title:'Dynasties and epic connection',paragraphs:[
        'Genealogies move from primordial kings toward familiar epic lineages. They situate Rama, Yadavas and other heroes inside one sacred history and allow later dynasties to be appended.',
        'Comparing Matsya lists with Vishnu, Vayu and Brahmanda reveals shared sources and redactional variation useful for Purana textual history.'
      ]},
      {title:'Vratas as sacred calendar',paragraphs:[
        'The Matsya’s many named vows demonstrate how medieval Purana religion organizes the year. Lunar date, deity, fast, bath, image and gift combine into recurring ritual technologies.',
        'The variety of vows also suggests accretion: new observances can be added without disturbing older genealogical or mythic sequence.'
      ]},
      {title:'Prayaga, Narmada and pilgrimage',paragraphs:[
        'Sacred rivers and confluences connect the Purana to the expanding culture of tirtha-mahatmya. Prayaga and Narmada are interpreted through myth, ritual promises and the power of bathing.',
        'Such chapters should be compared with Skanda, Brahma and other river texts because regional sacred geography often circulates between Purana titles.'
      ]},
      {title:'Architecture as cosmology made visible',paragraphs:[
        'Temple proportions and image rules can be read as efforts to materialize cosmic order. Measurement translates an ordered universe into built space, while consecration makes the designed object ritually alive.',
        'This connection explains why architecture belongs in a Purana rather than being random technical intrusion: building is another form of preserving and embodying sacred order.'
      ]},
      {title:'Future kings and the closing index',paragraphs:[
        'Later dynastic lists extend sacred history toward the redactor’s horizon. Like other Purana “future” genealogies, they can mix remembered political sequence with schematic decline.',
        'The final index-like chapter is a valuable internal guide to what the received editor considered the contents of the work, and it makes the Purana conscious of its own encyclopedic breadth.'
      ]}
    ]},
    {title:'Theology',paragraphs:[
      'Vishnu’s Matsya avatara frames the book, but the received theology is broadly Brahmanical. Shiva, Goddess, Surya and other deities receive substantial narrative and ritual attention.',
      'Preservation is a recurring theological motif. Vishnu preserves life through the flood; ritual preserves dharma; temple preserves divine presence; genealogy preserves sacred memory.',
      'Shaiva myths show that Vishnu framing does not require exclusion. Divine roles can be distributed across a shared cosmos.',
      'Technical knowledge itself becomes sacred when revealed within Purana transmission. Architecture and polity are not outside theology if they maintain ordered human worlds.',
      'Liberation remains the ultimate horizon despite the text’s intense interest in worldly order and merit.'
    ]},
    {title:'Influences and reception',paragraphs:[
      'The flood narrative participates in a very old South Asian Manu tradition and later became one of the standard ten-avatara stories of Vishnu. The Purana helps connect archaic flood memory to mature Vaishnava avatara theology.',
      'Architecture and iconography chapters have had enormous modern scholarly reception. Art historians use them to compare textual ideals with surviving temples, images and regional styles.',
      'Vrata materials contributed to the wider medieval ritual archive and could be cited or adapted by later compilers.',
      'Dynastic lists influenced historical reconstruction, though modern scholarship is more cautious about treating them as straightforward chronological records.',
      'Narmada and other pilgrimage materials participate in regional traditions also preserved under Skanda and other titles, demonstrating circulation of mahatmya literature.',
      'The Purana’s encyclopedic form helped establish the expectation that scripture might preserve technical knowledge essential to society as well as myth.'
    ]},
    {title:'Rites, dharma and social history',paragraphs:[
      'The Matsya is a major source for vrata, gift, śrāddha, royal duty and temple-building ideals. Its social range extends from household calendar to royal patronage and artisan production.',
      'Architectural chapters imply collaborative institutions of patron, priest, architect and craft specialist even when the Sanskrit voice privileges learned prescription.',
      'Raja-dharma and omen sections reveal an ideal of kingship in which political decision is inseparable from ritual timing and cosmic signs.',
      'Dāna chapters show wealth converted into religious merit through highly symbolic gifts. They are evidence for ideals of patronage rather than direct accounting records.',
      'Prescriptions for social order should be treated as normative. The encyclopedic text spans centuries, making it especially hazardous to infer one homogeneous “Matsya society.”'
    ]},
    {title:'Critical edition',paragraphs:[
      'The Matsya lacks a universally dominant complete stemmatic critical edition. Common 290/291-chapter prints are received witnesses containing material of multiple ages.',
      'Technical blocks should be collated against specialist traditions. Architecture especially requires comparison with Brihat Samhita, vastu and shilpa texts.',
      'Genealogical passages should be compared with Vayu, Vishnu and Brahmanda witnesses to identify shared and secondary readings.',
      'The final chapter’s internal contents summary provides a useful control on the received structure but does not prove every summarized block belongs to the earliest layer.'
    ]},
    {title:'Further reading',paragraphs:[
      'Rocher and Hazra provide overall textual history. Specialist art-historical work should be used for architecture, while genealogy and ritual sections require their own comparative literatures.',
      'The Purana rewards thematic reading because its long single sequence contains several distinct knowledge traditions whose histories cannot be solved by one dating argument.'
    ]}
  ],[{title:'Ludo Rocher — The Puranas',detail:'Standard Matsya survey.'},{title:'Studies on Architecture in the Matsya Purana',detail:'Specialist comparison of architectural chapters with Indian building traditions.'}]);

  add('Garuḍa Purāṇa',[
    {title:'Date of composition',paragraphs:[
      'The Garuda Purana is a medieval encyclopedic Vaishnava text whose popular identification with death rites represents only one part—and probably a later, highly unstable part—of its received corpus. The Purvakhanda’s broad subjects and the Pretakhanda’s funerary world have distinct textual histories.',
      'Traditional catalogues assign about nineteen thousand verses, while surviving manuscripts preserve substantially fewer, often around eight thousand depending recension. This gap indicates major textual change and makes any claim about an original full Garuda speculative.',
      'The Purvakhanda’s encyclopedic character resembles Agni and likely developed within a similar early-medieval culture of compendia. Iconography, medicine, gemology, grammar, polity, rites and yoga belong to different specialist histories and should be dated by comparison.',
      'The Pretakhanda or Pretakalpa is especially unstable. At least several markedly different recensions circulate, with chapter counts ranging roughly from the thirties into the high forties. Its modern prominence at funerals should not be used to date the rest of the Purana.',
      'A Brahmakhanda appears in some printed recensions, another sign that the boundaries of the “complete Garuda Purana” are not universal.'
    ]},
    {title:'Structure',paragraphs:[
      'The most common broad division is Purvakhanda and Uttarakhanda/Pretakhanda. Purvakhanda often contains around 229 chapters, though some recensions have roughly 240–243. Pretakhanda varies much more dramatically. Some Venkateshvara traditions append or distinguish a Brahmakhanda.',
      'Purvakhanda is an encyclopedia: Vishnu worship and avatara materials stand beside iconography, temple rites, medicine, toxicology, gems, astronomy, grammar, prosody, dharma, statecraft and yoga. Its structure resembles a sacred handbook rather than continuous myth.',
      'Pretakhanda shifts into the ritual and imaginative world of death: dying, cremation, preta state, Yama’s realm, post-mortem journey, offerings and transition to ancestor status. Repetition and recensional variation show long ritual use and adaptation.',
      'The structural imbalance matters. The much larger Purvakhanda is often ignored by modern readers because the smaller death section became culturally famous. A scholarly article must reverse that distortion.'
    ],subsections:[
      {title:'Purvakhanda — the encyclopedic Garuda',paragraphs:[
        'Vishnu teaches Garuda, who transmits knowledge onward. The divine bird’s role as traveller between worlds suits a text whose subjects move from theology to medicine, gems and liberation.',
        'The section’s breadth should be mapped by clusters rather than treated as miscellaneous trivia. Each cluster is a compact shastra inserted into a Vaishnava revelatory frame.'
      ]},
      {title:'Pretakhanda — death as a ritual process',paragraphs:[
        'The Pretakhanda narrates death not as an instant transition but as a process involving body disposal, preta condition, ritual offerings, journey and eventual ancestral integration. Time after death is organized by the rites of survivors.',
        'This made the section powerful in later funeral culture, but recensional instability means no single printed journey can be assumed universal Hindu doctrine.'
      ]},
      {title:'Brahmakhanda and supplementary transmission',paragraphs:[
        'Some editions include a distinct Brahmakhanda with its own theological material. Its variable presence demonstrates that the Purana’s outer boundary remained open.',
        'Supplements should be catalogued by manuscript and press rather than automatically incorporated into a hypothetical original.'
      ]}
    ]},
    {title:'Contents',paragraphs:[
      'The Garuda Purana joins two very different encyclopedias: one of living religious and practical knowledge, and one of dying, post-mortem transformation and ancestral ritual. Together they make the body—healthy, ritually marked, dying and posthumous—a major site of Purana thought.'
    ],subsections:[
      {title:'Vishnu, Garuda and sacred instruction',paragraphs:[
        'The dialogue makes Garuda more than a vehicle. As recipient and transmitter he becomes the narrative bridge through which Vishnu explains cosmic, ritual and practical knowledge.',
        'Vaishnava framing can encompass subjects that have no obvious sectarian content because all valid knowledge is located within divine order.'
      ]},
      {title:'Iconography and consecration',paragraphs:[
        'Image forms, measurements and worship connect Garuda to Agni and shilpa traditions. The chapters help reconstruct how deity form was classified and ritually installed.',
        'As elsewhere, the text is prescriptive rather than a universal record of workshop practice. Comparison with surviving images and Agamas is necessary.'
      ]},
      {title:'Ayurveda, poison and bodily knowledge',paragraphs:[
        'Medical and toxicological chapters make the body a practical object of sacred knowledge. Remedies, disease classification and poison treatment demonstrate encyclopedic ambition.',
        'Their historical value depends on comparison with Ayurveda proper. A Purana summary can preserve variant traditions or later popularizations.'
      ]},
      {title:'Gemology and material auspiciousness',paragraphs:[
        'Gem chapters classify stones, qualities, defects and effects. Material substances become carriers of value, health, status and cosmic correspondence.',
        'Such knowledge intersects trade and court culture as well as ritual. The Purana is therefore evidence for learned ideas about material objects, not only gods.'
      ]},
      {title:'Dharma, polity and ritual life',paragraphs:[
        'Rules of conduct, gifts, worship and political duty extend the encyclopedia into social order. The same text that treats gems and medicine also explains merit and kingship.',
        'This breadth reflects a worldview in which practical competence and religious order are not separate domains.'
      ]},
      {title:'Yoga and liberation',paragraphs:[
        'Yoga sections turn from maintaining embodied life to transcending embodied limitation. Breath, concentration, knowledge and devotion place practical chapters within a larger soteriological horizon.',
        'The juxtaposition with medicine is particularly revealing: preserving life and escaping rebirth are different but legitimate goals inside one Purana.'
      ]},
      {title:'The preta journey',paragraphs:[
        'The Pretakhanda gives a vivid account of the deceased moving through post-mortem states, encountering Yama’s agents and receiving consequences shaped by action and family ritual. Later summaries often present this as a universally fixed map, but textual variants are extensive.',
        'The narrative makes grief actionable for survivors. Offerings, cremation and periodic rites are not only commemorative; they are represented as materially affecting the condition of the dead.',
        'The famous year-long journey in some recensions organizes mourning and ritual time. Whether every Hindu funeral tradition followed the text is a separate historical question.'
      ]},
      {title:'From preta to pitr',paragraphs:[
        'Funerary rites aim to transform an unstable recently deceased being into an ancestor integrated with lineage. This is a social and cosmological transition: the dead person changes status through actions performed by the living.',
        'The process explains why inheritance, kinship and ritual competence become so important in funeral discourse. Death tests the continuity of the household.'
      ]}
    ]},
    {title:'Theology',paragraphs:[
      'The Purvakhanda is Vaishnava in frame and devotion but encyclopedic in content. Vishnu supplies ultimate authority while many gods and practical disciplines remain legitimate.',
      'The Pretakhanda’s theology is dominated by karma, Yama, ancestors and ritual mediation. Vishnu devotion may promise higher rescue, but ordinary post-mortem process remains highly elaborated.',
      'The coexistence of medicine and death theology produces a broad anthropology: embodied life is worth protecting, yet embodiment is impermanent and morally consequential.',
      'Grace and ritual do not simply cancel karma. The text describes a complex interaction among previous action, devotion and rites performed by survivors.',
      'Liberation teaching finally exceeds the ancestor cycle, offering a goal beyond successful transition to heavenly or pitṛ worlds.'
    ]},
    {title:'Influences and reception',paragraphs:[
      'The Garuda Purana’s modern reputation is dominated by funerary use. In many communities portions associated with death are read after a death, giving the text an emotional and ritual visibility far beyond its smaller Pretakhanda size.',
      'This reception can obscure the much larger encyclopedic Purvakhanda. Reintroducing medicine, gemology, iconography and yoga changes the reader’s sense of what kind of Purana Garuda actually is.',
      'The Garuda Purana Saroddhara, a later digest/commentary of death material, became highly influential and was translated early into English. Popular “Garuda Purana” descriptions sometimes derive more directly from this digest than from all recensions of the full Purana.',
      'Funeral priests and household traditions can use Garuda authority selectively. Actual rites vary regionally and do not always match any one Pretakhanda recension.',
      'Medical and gemological passages influenced modern histories of Indian science and material culture, though selective quotation must be controlled by edition.',
      'The text is therefore a case study in reception narrowing: one culturally intense use—death—can redefine an encyclopedic scripture in public memory.'
    ]},
    {title:'Rites, dharma and social history',paragraphs:[
      'Funeral ritual provides unusually detailed evidence for ideals of kinship, mourning and ancestral transition. The text assigns responsibility to survivors, making death a social process rather than a private event.',
      'The exact rites should not be generalized as pan-Indian practice. Pretakhanda recensions differ enormously, and ethnographic traditions can diverge from Sanskrit prescription.',
      'Dāna after death connects grief, merit and economic redistribution. Gifts to ritual specialists are represented as beneficial to the deceased, creating a sacred economy around mourning.',
      'Purvakhanda social material extends far beyond death through health, kingship, image worship and household conduct.',
      'The contrast between maintaining the body through medicine and disposing of it ritually after death makes the Garuda especially rich for histories of the religious body.'
    ]},
    {title:'Critical edition',paragraphs:[
      'The Garuda’s most urgent textual problem is Pretakhanda variation. Several substantially different versions circulate, and chapter counts vary enough that one “standard” post-mortem itinerary is a modern simplification.',
      'Purvakhanda also varies between roughly 229 and 240-plus chapters. A full critical edition would need to map the encyclopedic blocks independently.',
      'Saroddhara should be distinguished from the full Garuda Purana. It is an influential later digest, not a neutral reproduction of every manuscript.',
      'Brahmakhanda’s variable presence requires separate cataloguing. A “three-khanda Garuda” and a “two-khanda Garuda” are different received textual objects.',
      'Historical claims about funeral practice should cite the exact Sanskrit recension and, ideally, compare it with ethnographic or legal evidence.'
    ]},
    {title:'Further reading',paragraphs:[
      'Rocher is essential for the recensional problem. Scholarship on death, such as Jonathan Parry’s work, helps distinguish Sanskrit prescriptions from actual regional mortuary traditions.',
      'The Purvakhanda deserves subject-specific comparison with Ayurveda, gemology and iconography rather than being treated as preface to the Pretakhanda.'
    ]}
  ],[{title:'Ludo Rocher — The Puranas',detail:'Standard Garuda textual-history discussion.'},{title:'Jonathan Parry — Death in Banaras',detail:'Anthropological comparison useful for death practices and Sanskrit textual ideals.'},{title:'Garuda Purana Saroddhara, trans. Ernest Wood and S. V. Subrahmanyam',detail:'Influential later digest of death material; should be distinguished from full Purana recensions.'}]);

  add('Brahmāṇḍa Purāṇa',[
    {title:'Date of composition',paragraphs:[
      'The Brahmanda Purana contains an old Purana core closely related to the Vayu alongside major later additions whose independent character is structurally visible. The title and classical four-pada material belong to early Purana formation, while Lalitopakhyana and Adhyatma Ramayana traditions represent later Shakta and Rama-devotional growth.',
      'The close Vayu-Brahmanda parallels are among the strongest evidence for reconstructing a shared early Purana textual complex. Large sections of cosmology, genealogy and dynastic history correspond in sequence and wording, implying common ancestry or extensive redactional exchange.',
      'The Lalitopakhyana is demonstrably secondary to the four-pada conclusion in the received arrangement. It begins with a new benediction and new principal interlocutors, Agastya and Hayagriva, after the Upasamhara has already given the sort of epilogue that closes a Purana. Its Shakta theology should therefore be dated independently.',
      'The Adhyatma Ramayana likewise has its own literary and theological history and circulates independently. Its attachment to Brahmanda does not date the older Purana core, nor does the antiquity of Rama narrative date this Vedantic devotional reworking.',
      'The extant Brahmanda is consequently a layered archive whose different embedded works became more famous in later devotion than much of the host Purana itself.'
    ]},
    {title:'Structure',paragraphs:[
      'The classical core shares a four-pada logic with Vayu: Prakriya, Anushanga, Upodghata and Upasamhara. In common received organization these padas are distributed across larger bhagas, but the internal fourfold scheme remains the key to the older architecture.',
      'Prakriya treats creation and elemental process. Anushanga develops geography, kalpas, genealogies, ancestors and related classical Purana materials. Upodghata expands cosmic, dynastic and religious history. Upasamhara moves through dissolution and gives a formal conclusion.',
      'The fact that Upasamhara closes the Purana in form is critical when assessing what follows. Lalitopakhyana begins afresh with Agastya questioning Hayagriva, creating a literary seam stronger than a mere topical shift.',
      'The Lalita cycle itself is a large, internally organized Shakta work including the war against Bhandasura, Sri Vidya theology and Lalita Sahasranama. Its integration transformed the religious reception of the host Purana.',
      'Adhyatma Ramayana is another substantial embedded or associated work, reinterpreting the Rama story through a strongly devotional and nondual/Vedantic framework. Like Gautami in Brahma, it must be studied as both part of a received Purana and an independent textual tradition.'
    ],subsections:[
      {title:'The four-pada classical core',paragraphs:[
        'The shared four-pada structure with Vayu is one of the clearest clues to common textual ancestry. Creation, genealogies, manvantaras and dynastic history provide the classical five-lakshana Purana profile.',
        'This old core should not be interpreted through the later fame of Lalita alone. The Brahmanda was already a substantial cosmological and genealogical Purana before Shakta redaction transformed its reception.'
      ]},
      {title:'Lalitopakhyana',paragraphs:[
        'Agastya and Hayagriva open a new revelatory world. Lalita emerges as supreme Goddess, Bhandasura becomes the central antagonist and the Sri Chakra/Sri Vidya universe gives the section a ritual-theological specificity unlike the older four padas.',
        'The independent invocation and change of speakers function almost like a new book title embedded after the Purana’s ending. Textual criticism and literary structure point in the same direction.'
      ]},
      {title:'Adhyatma Ramayana',paragraphs:[
        'The Adhyatma Ramayana retells Rama not primarily as the ethically constrained prince of Valmiki but as fully conscious supreme divinity. Episodes become vehicles for Vedantic and bhakti teaching.',
        'Its independent manuscript and devotional life means it should be cited by its own book and chapter as well as by Brahmanda location when relevant.'
      ]}
    ]},
    {title:'Contents',paragraphs:[
      'The Brahmanda Purana’s contents make sense only when host and embedded works are distinguished. The older core is a cosmic-dynastic encyclopedia closely related to Vayu; the later famous additions are specialized scriptures of Lalita and Rama that reorient the Purana toward Shakta and Vaishnava devotional worlds.'
    ],subsections:[
      {title:'Creation and dissolution',paragraphs:[
        'The older core explains evolution from primordial principles and eventual involution at pralaya. Gunas, elements and cosmic cycles create a Samkhya-influenced framework shared across many Puranas.',
        'Upasamhara mirrors creation in reverse, giving the book a strong cosmological closure before later additions begin.'
      ]},
      {title:'Geography and cosmic order',paragraphs:[
        'Continents, oceans, mountains, worlds and celestial regions map the “Brahmanda,” the cosmic egg named by the title. The Purana’s name is therefore reflected in its large-scale interest in the structure of the universe.',
        'Bharata and human geography are nested inside this cosmic body, connecting regional action to universal structure.'
      ]},
      {title:'Genealogies and manvantaras',paragraphs:[
        'Sages, gods, Manus and royal lines provide temporal architecture. Comparison with Vayu reveals where two related traditions preserve common order and where each redactor modifies lineage.',
        'These chapters are major evidence for the history of Purana genealogy precisely because parallel witnesses permit textual control unavailable in isolated lists.'
      ]},
      {title:'Lalita’s emergence and Bhandasura',paragraphs:[
        'The Lalitopakhyana constructs a complete Goddess epic. Lalita emerges in response to cosmic crisis, organizes divine forces and defeats Bhandasura. Martial narrative becomes the vehicle for a sophisticated theology of supreme Shakti.',
        'Unlike the Devi Mahatmya’s relatively compact three-battle structure, the Lalita cycle is deeply connected to Sri Vidya ritual symbolism and a highly elaborated divine court.'
      ]},
      {title:'Lalita Sahasranama',paragraphs:[
        'The thousand names became one of the most widely recited Shakta liturgies in South Asia. Each name can function as praise, theological proposition and meditative seed.',
        'Its independent ritual life far exceeds ordinary readership of Brahmanda’s cosmological core. This is a striking example of an embedded unit becoming culturally more prominent than its host.'
      ]},
      {title:'Sri Vidya and sacred diagram',paragraphs:[
        'Lalita theology is inseparable from Sri Vidya’s ritual and cosmological mapping. Goddess, mantra, body, divine city and geometric diagram can correspond to one another.',
        'The Purana thus becomes a bridge between public narrative scripture and more specialized Shakta ritual systems.'
      ]},
      {title:'Adhyatma Ramayana — epic rewritten as theology',paragraphs:[
        'The Adhyatma Ramayana retains the broad Rama plot but interprets Rama’s identity explicitly through supreme Brahman and devotion. Characters can speak philosophical teachings absent or differently emphasized in Valmiki.',
        'The work is therefore not simply a shortened epic. It is a theological commentary recast as narrative and had major influence on later Rama devotion.'
      ]}
    ]},
    {title:'Theology',paragraphs:[
      'The older Brahmanda core is broadly Brahmanical and cosmological, revering multiple deities within a cyclical universe. It cannot be retroactively described as a pure Sri Vidya Purana because Lalita material belongs to a later appended work.',
      'Lalitopakhyana makes the Goddess supreme, integrating cosmic sovereignty, beauty, warfare, mantra and diagram. Shakti is not merely consort energy but the highest reality of the embedded text.',
      'The Sahasranama allows theological plurality through names: Lalita can be mother, ruler, consciousness, beauty, mantra and transcendent absolute without one epithet exhausting her.',
      'Adhyatma Ramayana provides a different supremacy, identifying Rama with ultimate Brahman and interpreting his apparently human acts as divine play or pedagogical appearance.',
      'The received Brahmanda therefore contains more than one developed sectarian theology. The correct historical response is stratification, not harmonization or choosing one as the “real” doctrine of the whole title.',
      'At the level of reception, however, readers can genuinely experience the bound work as a sacred unity. Textual layers and devotional unity are different analytical questions.'
    ]},
    {title:'Influences and reception',paragraphs:[
      'The Vayu-Brahmanda relationship has enormous influence on modern Purana scholarship because it offers a rare comparative window into early textual growth. Scholars can trace shared paragraphs, lists and structures across two canonical descendants.',
      'Lalita Sahasranama became one of the most influential Goddess hymns in living Hindu practice. Daily and festival recitation, commentary and ritual use give it an independent canonical status in Sri Vidya communities.',
      'Lalitopakhyana’s Bhandasura myth and divine-city imagery shaped Shakta narrative, ritual visualization and modern devotional publication.',
      'Adhyatma Ramayana became a major Rama scripture and influenced vernacular devotional traditions. Its explicit divine Rama helped shape later theological readings of the epic.',
      'The host Purana is therefore frequently encountered indirectly. A devotee may know Lalita Sahasranama or Adhyatma Ramayana intimately without ever reading Brahmanda creation and genealogy chapters.',
      'This reception pattern is historically instructive: “influence of a Purana” can actually mean influence of later embedded texts that became autonomous traditions.',
      'Modern critical scholarship restores the host/addition distinction without denying the religious authority acquired by the additions. Secondary textual status is not secondary cultural importance.'
    ]},
    {title:'Rites, dharma and social history',paragraphs:[
      'The older core contains the classical Purana world of ancestors, dynasties, cosmic cycles and ritual order. These materials reflect normative Brahmanical models shared with Vayu.',
      'Lalita material introduces a different ritual environment of mantra, Goddess worship, names and Sri Vidya symbolism. Specialized initiatory traditions intersect with public Purana narrative.',
      'Sahasranama recitation is especially portable: a highly elaborate theology can enter domestic and temple practice through repeated names without requiring mastery of the entire ritual system.',
      'Adhyatma Ramayana connects household and public Rama devotion with Vedantic theology, giving narrative recitation a soteriological role.',
      'The coexistence of these layers makes the Brahmanda poor evidence for one uniform historical society but excellent evidence for successive religious worlds using the same canonical title.'
    ]},
    {title:'Critical edition',paragraphs:[
      'A critical history must first establish the four-pada core in comparison with Vayu. Parallel passages can expose common ancestry and later divergence.',
      'Lalitopakhyana should be edited as an independent appended work as well as within Brahmanda manuscripts. Its fresh benediction and Agastya-Hayagriva frame are primary structural evidence of secondary attachment.',
      'Adhyatma Ramayana likewise possesses an independent manuscript tradition requiring its own textual criticism.',
      'Modern “complete Brahmanda” editions can therefore bind historically separate corpora. Completeness is bibliographic, not evidence of simultaneous composition.',
      'The Ancient Indian Tradition and Mythology translation is valuable for navigating the enormous received work, but detailed intertextual claims require Sanskrit collation.'
    ]},
    {title:'Further reading',paragraphs:[
      'Rocher and Hazra are foundational for the Vayu relationship and layered structure. The AITM introduction is useful for the four-pada architecture and internal evidence that Lalitopakhyana follows a completed Upasamhara.',
      'Lalita research should move into specialist Sri Vidya scholarship, while Adhyatma Ramayana should be compared with Valmiki and later Rama traditions. Treating those embedded works as independent corpora produces far deeper interpretation than a single generic Brahmanda summary.'
    ]}
  ],[{title:'Ludo Rocher — The Puranas',detail:'Standard Brahmanda/Vayu textual-history survey.'},{title:'The Brahmanda Purana, Ancient Indian Tradition and Mythology, vols. 22–26',detail:'Translation and extensive introduction to the four-pada core and Lalitopakhyana.'}]);
})();